(# What closes a booklet: the competition's logo beside the small print that says who built
    this copy, from what, and whose it is.

    It is the content of the last page's **footer** -- `base-booklet.jtex` boxes this once into
    `\dgsColophonBox`, the `colophon` page style puts that box above the page number, and
    `blocks/booklet/footer.jtex` reserves the box's own height under the credits. So this file
    is the band and nothing else: no rule, because the footrule is now the rule it used to draw
    for itself, and no page number, because that has to stay dynamic and cannot be frozen into
    a box built once in the preamble.

    **Two columns, one third and two thirds**, because the two halves want opposite things. The
    logo is a wordmark and wants height; the small print wants a measure it can set three short
    lines in. Side by side they are 20mm tall against about 11mm, so both boxes are `[c]` and
    centre on each other -- the logo is the taller and the text sits against its middle. The
    columns are `\textwidth/3` and the remainder, so they add to `\textwidth` exactly and the
    only air between them is what centring a 36mm logo in a 60mm column leaves, which is where
    a gutter would have gone anyway.

    The logo is the competition's own, the one the running head carries, and it is already a
    prerequisite of the document for that reason.

    The version of each repository is `git describe`, not the bare hash: on a tagged commit it
    is a name a reader can check out -- `29-final` -- and anywhere else it is the hash with the
    distance from the last tag in front of it, and `-dirty` behind it where the tree the build
    came from was not the tree the hash names. `blocks/metadata.jtex` carries the same facts
    into the file itself, with the exact hash alongside, for whoever has the PDF rather than
    the paper.

    **Three lines of one paragraph, not three paragraphs.** A `\par` anywhere inside the
    argument of a macro that is not `\long` ends the scan, and the box is built inside `\sbox`
    within `\AtBeginDocument`, neither of which is long -- so the rows are separated by `\\`,
    which carries no such restriction. `\raggedright` for the same reason it is right anywhere
    else: three lines of unequal length justified to one measure open rivers in 8pt type.

    **The file must not end in `%`.** Jinja drops the trailing newline of an include, so a
    comment character on the last line would swallow whatever the including template writes
    next -- which is the brace that closes the box, and the error for that arrives pages away
    as `File ended while scanning use of \AddToHook`. #)
\fontsize{8}{10}\selectfont
\parbox[c]{\dimexpr\textwidth / 3\relax}{%
    \centering
    \includegraphics[keepaspectratio = true, height = 20mm]%
        {build/(* module.id *)/(* competition.id *)/.static/logo/logo.pdf}%
}%
\parbox[c]{\dimexpr\textwidth - \textwidth / 3\relax}{%
    \raggedright
    (* i18n[language.id].competition.name.nominative *) (* volume.number *),
    (* language.id *) ((* language.name *)).
    \copyright{} (* competition.rights_holder *), (* volume.date.strftime('%Y') *).
    \href{https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/}{CC BY-NC-SA 4.0}\\
    booklet built by (* build.user *) with dgs \texttt{(* build.dgs.branch *)}: \texttt{(* build.dgs.describe *)}
    at (* build.timestamp.strftime('%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S') *)\\
    from branch \texttt{(* build.repo.branch *)}: \texttt{(* build.repo.describe *)}%
}
